Dependency injection is not about the container. It is about a class declaring what it needs instead of going and fetching it. A class that calls `new PaymentGateway()` or a static `getInstance()` has hard-wired one implementation into itself: you cannot substitute a fake in a test, you cannot swap a sandbox gateway in staging, and the class silently owns a lifecycle it has no business owning. Take the collaborator as a parameter and all three problems disappear — Spring is simply the thing that wires it up for you at startup.

Constructor injection is preferred for reasons that are mostly about the class, not about Spring:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;   // final: cannot be reassigned or left null
    private final OrderRepository orders;

    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway, OrderRepository orders) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
        this.orders = orders;
    }
}
```

The fields can be `final`, so the object is immutable and safe to publish across threads. The object is **impossible to construct in an invalid state** — there is no window where it exists with a null dependency. In a unit test you call the constructor with mocks and need no Spring context and no reflection at all, whereas field injection forces `@InjectMocks` or `ReflectionTestUtils` to reach into private fields. And the constructor signature is honest: when it grows to eight parameters, that ugliness is the design telling you the class does too much. Field injection hides exactly that signal. Since Spring 4.3 a single constructor needs no `@Autowired` annotation.

`singleton` is the default — one instance per application context, not per JVM and not per classloader. `prototype` gives a new instance on every lookup. In a web application you also get `request`, `session`, `application` and `websocket`.

The trap is that **injection happens once**. A singleton is created once at start-up, and its prototype dependency is resolved exactly once at that moment. The field then holds a single prototype instance forever, and every call uses the same object. Your "prototype" is a singleton with extra annotations — which is fine until the object carries per-call state, at which point requests start seeing each other's data.

The fixes, roughly in order of preference:

```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportBuilder> builders;  // ask per call

    public Report build(Spec spec) {
        return builders.getObject().build(spec);           // fresh instance each time
    }
}
```

`ObjectProvider` (or `ObjectFactory`) is explicit and testable. `@Lookup` on an abstract method has Spring generate the lookup for you. A scoped proxy — `@Scope(value = "prototype", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)` — makes the injected reference a proxy that fetches a new target per invocation, which is what `request` and `session` scoped beans always need since they must be injectable into singletons.

The question behind the question is usually simpler: if the object holds per-call state, it probably should not be a bean at all. Construct it with `new` inside the method and pass its collaborators in. Also remember the container does not track prototypes after handing them out, so a prototype holding a resource will never have its `@PreDestroy` called.

`@Component` — and its stereotypes `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller` — marks a class you own so component scanning registers it. Spring constructs it, so you get no say in how. `@Bean` marks a factory method inside a configuration class, and you write the construction code yourself. Use `@Bean` when you cannot annotate the class because you do not own it (an `ObjectMapper`, a `RestClient`, a third-party client), when construction needs logic, or when one class needs to be registered several times under different names with different configuration. The stereotypes are not merely decorative either: `@Repository` enables exception translation from vendor-specific `SQLException`s into Spring's `DataAccessException` hierarchy.

`@Configuration` is itself a `@Component`, but with one extra behaviour controlled by `proxyBeanMethods`, which defaults to `true`:

```java
@Configuration                       // proxyBeanMethods = true, "full" mode
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean ObjectMapper mapper() { return new ObjectMapper(); }
    @Bean Renderer renderer() { return new Renderer(mapper()); }   // returns the singleton
}
```

In full mode Spring CGLIB-proxies the configuration class and intercepts every `@Bean` method call, so the call to `mapper()` inside `renderer()` returns the container's singleton rather than constructing a second one. Set `proxyBeanMethods = false` — "lite" mode — and that interception disappears: `mapper()` is a plain Java call and you get **two** `ObjectMapper` instances, one of them unmanaged. In lite mode you must declare the dependency as a method parameter, `Renderer renderer(ObjectMapper mapper)`, which Spring resolves from the container.

Lite mode exists because the CGLIB subclass costs class generation at start-up. Spring Boot's own auto-configurations all use `proxyBeanMethods = false` for precisely that reason, and it is also required for GraalVM native images. It is a fine default for your own configuration as long as you take dependencies as parameters.
